Hi Kelly,

I'm definitely curious to hear what the experts say, but thought I would jot down my thoughts based on my own work (typically using JAGS to run Bayesian demographic models) for now. (I see that your message is dated 3 days ago - not sure if I missed it before, or if there was a delay in the listserv sending it out.)

I have not considered the "effective sample size" of the MCMC simulations to be particularly important. I simply use enough replicates/iterations to get the model to converge, based on the Gelman-Rubin statistics and a visual assessment of the plots. The number of iterations required to achieve convergence depends on the amount of data, variance within the data, and especially the complexity of the model. I report how many iterations I ran and how much I thinned them for a resulting number of saved iterations used to calculate the estimates. This is what I have usually seen in the literature, at least in my field.

I personally try to avoid referring to the number of iterations as a "sample size" of any kind. It is effectively the sample size of the posterior distribution, for calculating the standard error and so forth. However, that sample size (and thus the SE) is a fairly artificial concept because you can run as many iterations as you want (or have the computing resources for!), increasing your sample size toward infinity and decreasing your SE toward zero. This is why the SD, not the SE, is typically reported for MCMC analyses. I've run into some cases of colleagues/readers being confused about the sample size/SE issue, because if the number of iterations is your "sample size," then they might think that the number of data points used to inform the model is irrelevant, which is certainly not true.

Hope that helps, and I'll look forward to any additions/corrections from the list.

Cheers,

Emily

