Hi Kelly,

As with any discussion of sample sizes: "It all depends...". Let's call the result of a hugely long run the "target" value; shorter runs will be different, so Question 1: how big a difference would you consider "wrong"? You can't eliminate wrong answers entirely, so Question 2: how often can you afford to be wrong?

Rules of thumb: if you want the point estimate (mean or median) to be within 5% of the SD 95% of the time, you need n.eff around 2,000. For credible intervals, you need larger n.eff's, because the estimate is based on a small proportion of values in the tail. To mean the same criteria, you need n.eff >10,000. See the attachment for background. You can get away with smaller values if your tolerance for error is higher.

Those numbers apply to draws from a normal distribution. If the distribution is highly skewed, I suspect higher values are needed. Unfortunately these highly skewed posteriors also tend to have high autocorrelation and hence low n.eff.

HTH,  Mike.

